const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Does working harder actually get you promoted faster in India?',
    a: 'Working hard is the entry ticket, not the deciding factor. Once your output clears the bar for your level, promotion decisions lean heavily on who in the room already knows what you did and vouches for you. Research on promotion criteria consistently finds that visibility — being known for your work by the people who decide — outweighs sheer effort once basic competence is proven. Strong output that nobody senior hears about rarely converts into a faster promotion on its own.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the real difference between a mentor and a sponsor at work?',
    a: 'A mentor talks to you — giving advice, feedback, and encouragement. A sponsor talks about you — advocating for your name in rooms and calibration meetings you are not part of. HBR research by Herminia Ibarra and colleagues found that without active sponsorship, even high-potential employees are less likely to get promoted, and that sponsorship influence outweighs personality, education, and experience in promotion outcomes. Most people collect mentors easily; sponsors have to be earned through visible, credible work a senior leader is willing to stake their reputation on.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I know if my promotion is being deliberately delayed or if I am genuinely not ready?',
    a: 'Run three checks. First, ask your manager directly what specific, measurable gap is blocking the promotion, and get it in writing or a follow-up email. A genuine gap has a name — a skill, a scope, a rating threshold. Second, watch whether the goalposts move: if you close one gap and a new, previously unmentioned one appears, that is a pattern worth naming out loud, not silently accepting again. Third, check whether the block is structural — no open headcount or budget for the level this cycle — versus about you specifically. A real skill gap comes with a plan and a date. A vague, shifting answer without either is the clearer sign of delay.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When is the best time to ask for a promotion in India?',
    a: 'Time your ask to align with, not against, your company\'s calibration cycle. Most Indian companies run either an annual cycle (often tied to the April-March financial year) or a mix of quarterly check-ins feeding one annual calibration and budget round. Asking for a promotion right after calibration decisions are locked, or with no documented wins from the recent review window, wastes the timing. The stronger move is to have your key results, scope changes, and manager conversations documented at least one full cycle before the formal review, since recency bias means what happened in the last few weeks before a review often outweighs what happened months earlier.',
